Conceptually, React keeps a per-Fiber array of hook slots and an index that resets to `0` at the start of every render. Each call to `useState` reads and advances that index; the setter it returns is a **closure** that remembers which slot it owns.

```javascript
const React = (function () {
  let memory = [];
  let index = 0;

  return {
    render(Component) {
      index = 0; // reset before every render
      return Component();
    },
    useState(initialValue) {
      const slot = index;
      if (memory[slot] === undefined) memory[slot] = initialValue;

      const setState = (next) => {
        memory[slot] = typeof next === 'function' ? next(memory[slot]) : next;
      };

      index++;
      return [memory[slot], setState];
    },
  };
})();
```

This is **why** hook order must never change between renders — slot `0` has to mean the same piece of state every time, or the array shifts and every hook after the shift reads the wrong slot.

## Key takeaways

- Hooks are closures over React's internal per-Fiber hook array.
- The setter closes over the slot index, not the value — that's how it always writes to the right place.
- Call order is the only thing tying a hook call to its stored state.

The single most common real-world React bug. An effect (or any callback) captures a variable from the render it was created in, and never sees the updated value again.

```javascript
function Counter() {
  const [count, setCount] = useState(0);

  useEffect(() => {
    const timer = setInterval(() => {
      // BUG — closes over count === 0 forever, from render #1
      setCount(count + 1);
    }, 1000);
    return () => clearInterval(timer);
  }, []); // empty deps → this closure is never recreated

  return <div>{count}</div>;
}
```

**Walk through it:** Render #1: `count` is 0, the interval callback closes over that 0. Every tick calls `setCount(0 + 1)` — state becomes 1, then stays 1 forever, because the *old* callback keeps reading the *old* 0.

### Fix 1 — functional update (preferred for timers/subscriptions)

```javascript
setInterval(() => setCount(prev => prev + 1), 1000);
```

### Fix 2 — put the value in the dependency array

```javascript
useEffect(() => {
  const timer = setInterval(() => setCount(count + 1), 1000);
  return () => clearInterval(timer);
}, [count]); // re-subscribes every time count changes — heavier, but also correct
```

## How to answer in the interview

> "A stale closure is an effect from an old render still reading old state, because it was never told to re-run. Fix it with a functional updater, or by listing the dependency so the effect is recreated with fresh values."

Both hooks run **after** React commits DOM changes, but at different points relative to the browser paint.

| | `useLayoutEffect` | `useEffect` |
| --- | --- | --- |
| When | Synchronously after DOM mutation, **before paint** | Asynchronously **after paint** |
| Blocks paint? | Yes | No |
| Typical use | DOM measurements, synchronous layout fixes | Data fetching, subscriptions, logging |

```javascript
// Positioning a tooltip — must be useLayoutEffect, or the user sees it jump from (0,0)
useLayoutEffect(() => {
  const anchor = anchorRef.current.getBoundingClientRect();
  setPos({ x: anchor.left, y: anchor.bottom + 8 });
}, [anchorRef]);
```

## How to answer in the interview

> "Default to useEffect. Reach for useLayoutEffect only when you measure or mutate layout and skipping it would cause a visible flash."

Hooks rely on a **stable call order** across renders. React stores hook state in an internal array indexed by call position.

1. Only call hooks at the **top level** — never inside a condition, loop, or nested function.
2. Only call hooks from **React function components** or **custom hooks**.

```javascript
// WRONG — hook index shifts between renders depending on `show`
function Bad({ show }) {
  if (show) {
    const [name] = useState(''); // sometimes slot 0, sometimes doesn't exist
  }
  const [count] = useState(0); // slot shifts between renders — corrupts state
}

// CORRECT — hooks unconditional; branch after
function Good({ show }) {
  const [name] = useState('');
  const [count] = useState(0);
  if (!show) return null;
  return <div>{name} {count}</div>;
}
```

Enforced at build time by `eslint-plugin-react-hooks` (`rules-of-hooks`, `exhaustive-deps`) — turn it on, it catches most of the Gotcha Bank entries before you ever run the app.

A custom hook is only worth being a hook if it calls other hooks. If a piece of logic is pure computation — formatting, sorting, validation — it should be a plain function, not a `useSomething`. Mixing the two either breaks the rules of hooks, or couples code to React for no reason and makes it harder to unit test.

```javascript
// Pure helper — no hooks, trivially testable
function generateRandomTasks(count) { /* ...pure computation... */ }

// Custom hook — owns state + effects, composes other hooks
function useTasks(initialCount = 5) {
  const [tasks, setTasks] = useState(() => generateRandomTasks(initialCount));
  const addTask = useCallback((title) => {
    setTasks(prev => [...prev, { id: crypto.randomUUID(), title, done: false }]);
  }, []);
  return { tasks, addTask };
}
```

## How to answer in the interview

> "Extract pure logic to helpers, stateful orchestration to hooks. Custom hooks compose other hooks; helpers are framework-agnostic and don't need renderHook to test."

`React.memo` shallow-compares props and skips re-rendering when they're all equal. It is **useless** the moment you hand it a freshly-allocated object, array, or arrow function as a prop — which happens by default on every render.

```javascript
function Parent() {
  const [count, setCount] = useState(0);
  const data = { name: 'Alice' }; // new object every render!
  return (
    <>
      <button onClick={() => setCount(c => c + 1)}>{count}</button>
      <Child data={data} /> {/* memo is defeated — new reference each time */}
    </>
  );
}
// Fix: stabilize the reference
const data = useMemo(() => ({ name: 'Alice' }), []);
```

A custom comparator is the second argument — and it's inverted from `shouldComponentUpdate`: return `true` to **skip** the render.

## Key takeaways

- `React.memo` shallow-compares props; a new object/array/function reference always fails that check.
- Stabilize props with `useMemo`/`useCallback`, or pass primitives, to make memoization actually work.
- A custom comparator returning `true` means "skip render" — the opposite of `shouldComponentUpdate`.

Rendering 10,000 rows means 10,000 real DOM nodes. Virtualization renders only the rows in the viewport (plus a small overscan buffer) inside a spacer element sized to the full scroll height — the classic "sliding window" pattern.

```javascript
// Fixed-height concept: only the visible slice mounts
const start = Math.floor(scrollTop / itemHeight);
const end = start + visibleCount + 1; // +1 row of overscan
const slice = items.slice(start, end);
```

In production reach for `react-window`, `react-virtualized`, or the headless `@tanstack/react-virtual` — don't hand-roll unless it's the interview question itself. Variable-height rows need a measure-then-correct strategy; fixed-height rows are pure arithmetic.

```javascript
const HeavyChart = React.lazy(() => import('./HeavyChart'));

function Dashboard() {
  return (
    <Suspense fallback={<Spinner />}>
      <HeavyChart />
    </Suspense>
  );
}
```

The fallback renders while the dynamic `import()` promise is in flight — that's a code *download*, not a data fetch. Split at route boundaries first (biggest win, users only pay for the route they're on), then at heavy, rarely-used components (modals, charts, rich text editors).

A function that takes a component and returns a new, enhanced component. Pre-hooks, this was the primary way to share logic (`withRouter`, `connect` from Redux).

```javascript
function withLoading(Component) {
  return function Wrapped({ isLoading, ...props }) {
    if (isLoading) return <Spinner />;
    return <Component {...props} />;
  };
}
const UserListWithLoading = withLoading(UserList);
```

## Gotcha

Wrapper hell (deeply nested displayNames make debugging painful) and prop-name collisions between HOCs. Custom hooks replace most HOC use cases today — reach for an HOC mainly when you must wrap the returned **element** itself, not just share stateful logic.

Refs don't pass through function components by default — `forwardRef` lets a parent reach a child's underlying DOM node or a curated imperative API.

```javascript
const FancyInput = React.forwardRef(function FancyInput(props, ref) {
  const innerRef = useRef(null);
  useImperativeHandle(ref, () => ({
    focus: () => innerRef.current?.focus(),
    clear: () => { innerRef.current.value = ''; },
  }));
  return <input ref={innerRef} {...props} />;
});
```

`useImperativeHandle` exposes a limited, intentional API instead of the raw node — use it for design-system primitives that parents must occasionally control imperatively (focus management, modals, form libraries).

```javascript
function Modal({ children }) {
  return createPortal(
    <div className="modal">{children}</div>,
    document.getElementById('modal-root')
  );
}
```

## Mental model

A portal renders into a *different DOM node* but stays in the *same React tree*. That's the part that trips people up: events still bubble through the React component tree (not the DOM tree), and Context from the logical parent still works inside the portal — only the physical DOM placement changed. Use it for modals, tooltips, and toasts that need to escape an `overflow: hidden` or `z-index` ancestor.

```javascript
class ErrorBoundary extends React.Component {
  state = { hasError: false };
  static getDerivedStateFromError() { return { hasError: true }; }
  componentDidCatch(error, info) { logToService(error, info); }
  render() {
    return this.state.hasError ? <Fallback /> : this.props.children;
  }
}
```

## Scope — this is a favorite trick question

Error boundaries only catch errors during **rendering, lifecycle methods, and constructors** of the tree beneath them. They do **not** catch errors in event handlers, async code (`setTimeout`, promises), SSR, or errors thrown in the boundary itself. There is still no hook equivalent — it must be a class component (or a library like `react-error-boundary` wrapping one for you).

Context has **no selective subscription**. Every consumer re-renders when the Provider's `value` changes, even one that only reads a single unrelated field.

```javascript
const AppContext = createContext({ user: null, theme: 'light', cart: [] });

function UserName() {
  const { user } = useContext(AppContext);
  return <span>{user?.name}</span>;
  // re-renders when theme OR cart changes too — not just user
}
```

1. **Split contexts by update frequency** — separate `ThemeContext`, `UserContext`, `CartContext` instead of one bag.
2. **Memoize the Provider value** — `useMemo(() => ({ user, theme }), [user, theme])`; without it, `value={{ user, theme }}` is a new object every parent render.
3. **Split state from dispatch** — a `dispatch` function reference is stable forever, so components that only dispatch don't need to re-render on state changes.
4. **Reach for an external store with selectors** (Zustand, Jotai) once updates are frequent or granular enough that splitting contexts stops being enough.

"Concurrent" means the render phase can be paused, thrown away, or resumed — not that anything runs literally in parallel on multiple threads.

| Feature | What it does |
| --- | --- |
| `createRoot` | New root API (replaces `ReactDOM.render`), required to unlock concurrent features |
| Automatic batching | Multiple `setState` calls in timeouts, promises, and native handlers now batch into one render — not just inside React event handlers |
| `useTransition` | Mark a state update as non-urgent / interruptible |
| `useDeferredValue` | Defer a received value until urgent work finishes |
| Streaming SSR | Send HTML in chunks; Suspense boundaries stream in as they resolve |

```javascript
const [isPending, startTransition] = useTransition();

function handleSearch(query) {
  setInputValue(query); // urgent — update the input immediately
  startTransition(() => {
    setResults(expensiveFilter(allItems, query)); // non-urgent — interruptible
  });
}
```

## Mental model

React can hold **two** versions of the UI in memory — the current committed tree and a work-in-progress tree. An urgent update (typing) can interrupt a slow re-render (filtering 50,000 rows) mid-flight; the commit phase itself is still atomic and synchronous once it starts.

Use `useTransition` when *you* trigger the state update. Use `useDeferredValue` when you only *receive* a value (a prop) and can't wrap its setter yourself.

```javascript
const deferredQuery = useDeferredValue(query);
const isStale = query !== deferredQuery;
const results = useMemo(() => filter(items, deferredQuery), [items, deferredQuery]);
```

| Behavior in development only | Purpose |
| --- | --- |
| Double-invokes render functions | Catch impure render logic — mutations, random values, side effects during render |
| Double-invokes effects (mount → unmount → remount) | Verify cleanup functions actually clean up |
| Warns on deprecated APIs | `findDOMNode`, legacy context, string refs |

```javascript
useEffect(() => {
  console.log('mounted');
  return () => console.log('cleanup');
}, []);
// StrictMode dev logs: mounted → cleanup → mounted
```

Production builds never double-render. Never rely on an effect running only once in development — write effects that are safe to mount, unmount, and remount immediately.

System-design-flavored React questions usually land here: how do you let independent teams ship independently without shipping a broken product.

| Composition approach | Pros | Cons |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Module Federation (Webpack 5) | Runtime sharing, shared deps | Build complexity, version skew |
| iframes | Strong isolation | Poor UX integration, routing pain |
| Web Components | Standards-based | Framework interop quirks |
| Monorepo + packages | Simpler operations | Coupled deploys unless deliberately avoided |

```javascript
// Host webpack.config.js
new ModuleFederationPlugin({
  name: 'host',
  remotes: { checkout: 'checkout@http://localhost:3001/remoteEntry.js' },
  shared: { react: { singleton: true }, 'react-dom': { singleton: true } },
});

// Host component
const RemoteCart = React.lazy(() => import('checkout/Cart'));
```

The recurring hard problems are the same four every time: shared state (an event bus, the URL, or a small shared store), routing ownership (host owns top-level routes, remotes own sub-routes), a shared design system so the seams don't show, and enforcing a single React instance via `shared: { singleton: true }` to avoid the "two Reacts" hooks-crash class of bug.
